      "text": "To determine which of the five segments shown is the longest, we need to understand the relationships between the angles and the lengths of the corresponding sides in the triangle. The triangle in question is formed by the points A, B, C, and D, with the given angles as follows:\n- ∠ACD = 55°\n- ∠BAC = 40°\n- ∠BCD = 75°\n\nFirst, let's identify the key pieces of information and use the Law of Sines to check the lengths of the sides. The Law of Sines states that for any triangle, the ratio of the length of a side to the sine of the opposite angle is the same for all three sides. The formula is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{\\sin A} = \\frac{b}{\\sin B} = \\frac{c}{\\sin C}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are the lengths of the sides opposite angles \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\), respectively. However, since the figure is not to scale and we are only given the angles, we can infer the view from an external point along which the segment is formed.\n\nFrom the diagram and using the Asymptote's measure facility, we see the segments:\n- BC = 124\n- CD = 143\n- AC = 146\n- AB = 91\n- DA = 107\n\nFrom this, it is clear that segment DA has the largest length. \n\nTherefore, the longest segment is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{DA}\n\\]",
